# dartman-game-setup-and-win-conditions

Verbatim Dartman game system: piece notation, starting positions, win conditions, and 8x8 board rendering format required to correctly set up and track a game.

## Procedures
- 1. Use these piece notations exactly — Player 1: P1 (Dartman), X1 (1x1 Deflector), X2 (1x2 Deflector, two coords); Player 2: P2 (Dartman), Y1 (1x1 Deflector), Y2 (1x2 Deflector, two coords).
- 2. Starting layout: P1(a,8), X1(a,7), X2(b,8)+(b,7); P2(h,1), Y1(h,2), Y2(g,1)+(g,2).
- 3. Win condition for Player 1: P1(g,2), X1(h,2), X2(g,1)+(g,2). Win condition for Player 2: P2(b,7), Y1(b,8), Y2(a,8)+(a,7).
- 4. Render the board as an 8x8 markdown table. Columns are A–H left to right; rows are 8–1 top to bottom. Empty squares are '.'; occupied squares show the piece shorthand (e.g. P1, X2, Y1). The 1x2 Deflector (X2 or Y2) occupies two cells in the same column (vertical orientation only).
- 5. Players alternate turns; each turn one piece moves once. Player 1 moves first unless otherwise specified.

## Conventions
- Piece shorthands (P1, X1, X2, P2, Y1, Y2) must be used verbatim — do not substitute generic names.
- The 1x2 Deflector always occupies two squares and must be listed with both coordinates.
- Columns are lowercase letters a–h; rows are numbers 8 (top) through 1 (bottom) — same as algebraic chess notation.

